	abstract = {Unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)-aided maritime communication is recognized as a key component of the sixth generation (6G) space-air-ground-sea integrated network architecture. However, traditional UAV-aided maritime channel models lack exploration of the millimeter-wave (mmWave) band and special weather conditions. To address this limitation, a novel mmWave channel model for UAV to unmanned surface vehicle (USV) communication is proposed in this letter. The impact of sea waves and evaporation ducts on channel in terms of clusters is incorporated into the model. Additionally, a joint space-timefrequency (STF) probability density function is introduced to characterize the non-stationarity of the channel across different domains. Furthermore, a rain attenuation model is integrated to investigate the effects of adverse weather on UAV-aided maritime communication. The proposed model is designed for mmWavebased maritime communication scenarios and is expected to provide a theoretical foundation for future research on 6G maritime communication.},
