	abstract = {Since the identification of long-range dependence in network traffic ten years ago, its consistent appearance across numerous measurement studies has largely discredited Poissnnhased models. However, since that original data set was collected, both link speeds and the number of Internet-connected hosts have increased by more than three orders of magnitude. Tbns, we now revisit the Poisson assumption, hy studying a combination of h i s torical traces and new measurements obtained from a major hackhone link belonging to a Tier 1 ISP. We show that unlike the older data sets, current network traffic can he well represented hy the Poisson model for suh-second time scales. At multi-second scales, we find a distinctive piecewise-linear non-stationarity, together with evidence of long-range dependence. Combining our ohservations across both time scales leads to a time-dependent Poisson Characterization of network traffic that, when viewed acrms very long time scales, exhibits the observed long-range dependence. This traffic characterization reconciliates the seemingly contradicting ohservations of Poisson and long-memory traffic characteristics. I t also seems to he in general agreement with recent theoretical models for large-scale traffic aggregation.},
